    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d54">

                <pb facs="0167" n="83" xml:id="pg83"/>

                <head>No. 53.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Denby to Mr. Bayard.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1886-04-19"><hi rendition="#italic">April</hi> 19, 1886</date>.
                        (Received June 28.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 132.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: As a further acknowledgment of your
                    dispatch No. 45, of the 4th March last, touching the claim of John P. Cowles,
                    jr., of Foochow, for reduction of duty on damaged exports, I have the honor to
                    state that no actual case has as yet arisen, but if one should arise I will
                    insist on compliance with the views stated in your dispatch of which the
                    Tsung-li Yamên is aware by a note which I sent it on the 13th December, 1885, a
                    copy of which I now inclose.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>CHARLES DENBY.</signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d54-a1.xml" xpointer="d54-a1"/>
            </div>
